00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.433
RON HICKS: HerdX is a fabulous
idea, if I can say that.

00:07:34.433 --> 00:07:35.850
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Self-proclaimed.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:39.290
RON HICKS: I'm somewhat
biased, but I will be honest.

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:41.020
I came out of
retirement to do what

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:45.110
I hoped would be a change for
livestock across the world.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.260
One was to become an
environmentally friendly

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.900
solution-- not easy to
address, but something we

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:51.250
feel like we've accomplished.

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:53.250
So HerdX is a
third-party provider,

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:56.530
a way in which we can take
livestock, improve the system,

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:59.410
and bring it to a consumer,
and improve the transparency.

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:01.172
That's basically HerdX.

00:08:01.172 --> 00:08:03.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So that's
a pretty broad statement.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.217
What does that actually entail,
for the livestock especially?

00:08:06.217 --> 00:08:07.550
RON HICKS: So it's a big change.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.690
So livestock in general
is, I would call,

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.880
less transparent by design.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.960
They're typically animals raised
in areas that are very, very

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.618
isolated, and by design, it
has to be because they're out

00:08:19.618 --> 00:08:20.660
in the middle of nowhere.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:22.150
So they're somewhat hidden.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.100
In general, there are
pretty good ranches

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:25.750
out there, pretty good feedlots.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.480
And they have no real
way of broadcasting

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.460
a positive performance animal.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:33.585
So the goal for HerdX is to
authenticate that through data.

00:08:33.585 --> 00:08:34.960
GABE WEISS: So
how does it do it?

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:36.307
Like, I first talked to you--

00:08:36.307 --> 00:08:38.140
I looked in my email
to find out exactly how

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.348
long we've been chatting--
it's been over a year now.

00:08:40.348 --> 00:08:43.210
So June of 2018 was
our first contact.

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:45.910
And at the time,
you were describing

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:47.080
the vision part of it.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:50.560
But mechanically, what
is it about livestock?

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:52.360
You and I have had a
lot of conversations

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:54.450
about what it does.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.160
And we know part of it is
that transparency to even all

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.822
the way to the end consumer.

00:08:58.822 --> 00:08:59.780
So how does it do that?

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.363
That's what we're
kind of looking for,

00:09:01.363 --> 00:09:02.920
is, what does the system do?

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.128
RON HICKS: So there would
probably be steps in there.

00:09:05.128 --> 00:09:09.230
I think the initial approach is
what was working 50 years ago?

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:10.630
What worked 100 years ago?

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.120
And if you look back,
you'll see kind of an art

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.480
to raising animals, even in a
more quarantined or confined

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:17.050
area.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:18.460
And it was this pen rider.

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.650
You had this gentleman
that would show up--

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.420
long-sleeved shirt.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:25.570
He was kind of the premier dude
of the day, and he'd come in

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:27.160
and he would cut
through cattle--

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.820
his goal to find animals that
were moving in somewhat of a

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.170
what we would call an anomaly.

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:33.712
If he could separate
that one animal,

00:09:33.712 --> 00:09:36.610
then he could determine
that that animal may need

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:38.620
some additional assistance.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:41.645
Maybe check and see if there's
a temp, some other things.

00:09:41.645 --> 00:09:42.770
They don't have that today.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.830
So if you look for
a pen rider, it's

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:47.080
the rarest of the cattle raise--

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:48.520
what I would call key--

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:49.810
to good animals.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:52.800
What we're doing at HerdX is
we're replacing that with data.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.550
GABE WEISS: So the pen
rider was a person.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:54.940
RON HICKS: It was,
riding on a horse.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:55.840
GABE WEISS: It was a
physical person that

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:58.570
would go down into the herds,
and walk amongst the animal,

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:01.210
and be like, all right, this
cow or this lamb or whatever,

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:02.830
they're acting a little strange.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:04.760
Let's pull them out of the
herd and let's do something.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:05.900
So that's been
replaced now by, what?

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:07.060
Just mass antibiotics, right?

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:08.800
Like, that's the answer,
is we'll just pre-medicate

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:09.460
all of our animals.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:09.760
RON HICKS: Absolutely.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:10.880
GABE WEISS: And then who
cares if they're sick?

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:11.360
It doesn't matter.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.220
They're not going
to affect anything

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:13.480
because they're all medicated.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:14.050
RON HICKS: They're medicated.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:15.633
You're not going to
cross-contaminate.

00:10:15.633 --> 00:10:17.440
It's an easy
management decision.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.832
Again, there's less
information, so you cover it.

00:10:20.832 --> 00:10:22.540
I think the beauty of
a HerdX type system

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:24.820
is you show an
animal's movement.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.430
If that anomaly occurs,
you go back to, basically,

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:29.858
in time, a more natural
method, and you've

00:10:29.858 --> 00:10:31.025
automated the entire system.

00:10:31.025 --> 00:10:31.290
GABE WEISS: Nice.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.310
So brass tacks now, you
talk about the HerdX

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:34.540
automated system.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:35.320
RON HICKS: Mm-hmm.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:37.640
GABE WEISS: I don't want to
infringe upon any IP here,

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.182
so if there's company secrets,
I don't want to touch on that.

00:10:40.182 --> 00:10:42.040
But what is the HerdX system?

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:43.830
So there's no pen
man anymore, right?

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:44.830
RON HICKS: That's right.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:45.010
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah.

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:46.610
GABE WEISS: So what
does the system do?

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.485
AUSTIN ADAMS: So we have
monitoring solutions

00:10:48.485 --> 00:10:50.590
that are deployed out
of the feedlot that

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:54.580
are able to monitor the animals'
movement to food and water.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:56.800
And through that, with
different algorithms

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.900
in the processing of
their movement data

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.150
and their movement
to water or to food,

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:03.070
as opposed to each other,
we're able to detect

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:04.240
who's the anomaly.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:06.370
And as that system
grows, we're also

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:10.510
able to kind of forecast
anomalies in different breeds

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:12.843
and in different weather
scenarios and things like that.

00:11:12.843 --> 00:11:14.927
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What's
an example of an anomaly?

00:11:14.927 --> 00:11:16.840
AUSTIN ADAMS: So an
example of an anomaly,

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.630
once you understand the
supply chain of cattle,

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:24.370
is many animals are coming
across the border from Mexico

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:27.160
or across several
states on trucks.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:29.080
And they were weaned
off of their mother

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:32.262
so they're off of milk
and onto water and grain.

00:11:32.262 --> 00:11:33.970
So they're just a big
transition in life,

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:35.570
so there's a lot of stress.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:39.040
So those animals might not be
exhibiting a temperature, which

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:42.800
is a fever or indicative of some
immune response in the animal,

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:44.740
which is trying to fight
a bacterial infection

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:46.780
or some stress
response in the animal.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:49.030
But they might be
moving weirdly,

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:52.253
meaning they might spend
a lot of time laying down,

00:11:52.253 --> 00:11:53.920
or they might not go
to the water trough

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:55.217
as often as other people.

00:11:55.217 --> 00:11:56.800
Or they might not
go to food as often.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.990
And there's a lot
of noise in there,

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:00.740
based on temperature
and things like that.

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:03.940
So that's why the pen rider
was kind of an art form,

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:07.990
because it was a human brain
able to filter out the noise

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:10.700
and understand, this is
an animal that needs help.

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:13.325
I can see by the way its
lips look that it needs water

00:12:13.325 --> 00:12:14.950
and it hasn't gone
to the water trough.

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:17.460
Because they can't watch them
all day like our systems can.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.147
So our system
doesn't need to know

00:12:19.147 --> 00:12:20.730
what the temperature
of the animal is.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:22.800
We can actually
see their movement

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:25.260
and decide a couple of
days before they even

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:27.330
have a temperature
that this animal needs

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:28.920
to be pulled and taken care of.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:30.780
And what taken care
of can mean is just

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.240
put on some lower
stress sort of behavior,

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.240
meaning get them away from
other animals for a while.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:39.030
Give them the actual
load of antibiotics

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.730
maybe that they actually
need, so not a mass treat,

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:44.822
but a specific acute
sort of treatment.

00:12:44.822 --> 00:12:47.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So collecting
a tremendous amount of data

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:48.960
per animal, and then
you multiply that

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:50.710
by the number of
animals, and you probably

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:54.000
end up seeing a lot of gigs
or terabytes of just raw data

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:55.890
and then using that
to make predictions

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:58.020
and understanding that
something's off here.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:00.810
Let's treat that specific case
with a much more strategic

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:03.780
implementation, rather than
just kind of a broad hey,

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.935
let's just throw a bunch
of pills at the cows

00:13:05.935 --> 00:13:06.810
and see what happens.

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:07.160
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:07.810
Yeah.

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:09.480
GABE WEISS: The prediction--
are you actually using

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:11.280
machine learning and artificial
intelligence to do that?

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.153
You're actually training
all of the models?

00:13:13.153 --> 00:13:15.570
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah, and that's
part of where GCP comes in,

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:18.150
is we've been able to
throw large volumes

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:23.430
of that behavioral data into
systems like BigQuery and Cloud

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.550
Dataflow, and able to
run models over that

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:30.030
and also test different models
to see what sort of negatives

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:31.770
they would generate
to understand

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:34.800
how we can continue to grow
and learn this thing over time,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.650
as the system gets smarter
with putting in breed data,

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:40.320
putting in other different
variables that could affect

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.420
the outcome of the animal,
which are all things

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:44.777
that these feedlots are
trying to do with their food.

00:13:44.777 --> 00:13:47.110
But they're not looking at
it from a health perspective.

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:48.777
They're going out of
their way to decide

00:13:48.777 --> 00:13:51.690
what's the most effective
food so that they can spend

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:54.755
the least amount of food for the
largest amount of weight gain.

00:13:54.755 --> 00:13:56.130
Because that's
what a feedlot is.

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:56.330
GABE WEISS: Totally.

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:58.110
AUSTIN ADAMS: And
it sounds horrible,

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:00.382
but really, it can be
done in a very humane way.

00:14:00.382 --> 00:14:02.590
And there's a lot of them
that do it in a humane way,

00:14:02.590 --> 00:14:05.515
but it gets hidden by
one bad actor, so--

00:14:05.515 --> 00:14:07.140
GABE WEISS: It's all
trade-offs, right?

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:09.240
You're trading off-- as harsh
as this is going to sound,

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.310
but you're trading off a
certain amount of humanity

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.090
for, really, the business.

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:13.798
I mean, ultimately,
they're all ranchers.

00:14:13.798 --> 00:14:15.340
They're all in it
for their business.

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:16.290
That's what they do.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:18.710
So I can imagine that there's
a lot of kind of trade-offs

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:19.470
when you're talking about that.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:22.110
So the cool thing for me, and
why I got interested in HerdX

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:24.300
when we first chatted,
was this feels like a way

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:25.980
to mitigate that trade-off.

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:27.900
You can still be humane
with these animals,

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:30.970
but still get the gains
and the profitability

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:33.190
so you can, like you said,
transform the industry.

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:35.190
That's kind of the idea,
is how can you do that.

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:37.380
So when did you know
you had something?

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:38.220
You had this idea.

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:40.290
You came out of
retirement for it.

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:42.870
What was the magical
moment where you said,

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:44.100
this could work?

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:45.660
We've got something here.

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:46.530
RON HICKS: I think--

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.460
well, you were actually involved
somewhat in that early stage.

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:51.720
We took over a small
feedlot, leased it,

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:54.600
decided just like any disruptive
product, which is really

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.430
my past, in order
to change a mindset,

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:57.520
you've got to prove it.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.870
And so here I'm coming in,
really unbiased, no background

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:01.860
on raising livestock.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.420
In fact, to be
honest with you, I'm

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:06.360
not someone that really likes
something that's not clean.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:07.020
Ask my wife.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:09.340
I mean, everything is
sterile at my home.

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:11.580
And so moving out
to a feedlot area,

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:15.780
leasing something from a very
famous style set of leaders

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:17.730
that, in the past, have
done very, very well,

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:18.970
this place was abandoned.

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:20.060
So we went in and
said, OK, we're

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:20.760
going to bring animals in.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:21.593
We're going to test.

00:15:21.593 --> 00:15:24.150
We're going to try some
things, all with natural means,

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:25.080
using data.

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:28.140
And the woman that was
there was watching this

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:31.462
and leased this property to us
started to become very excited.

00:15:31.462 --> 00:15:32.920
Again, when I first
went out there,

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:34.410
she probably thought
we were nuts.

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:36.707
And she began to say,
look, this is interesting.

00:15:36.707 --> 00:15:38.290
GABE WEISS: Was she
a rancher herself?

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:39.707
RON HICKS: She was
a past rancher,

00:15:39.707 --> 00:15:43.433
so they had basically given up
on the process of the feedlots.

00:15:43.433 --> 00:15:44.850
A lot of the family
had moved out.

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:46.230
That's also very common.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.027
The younger generation
decides to move to the city

00:15:49.027 --> 00:15:49.860
and do other things.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:52.600
So you'll see that older
generation trying to hang on,

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.540
but how do you do that
without maybe the labor

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:56.250
force or the means?

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.410
So we went in and just
decided we'll just

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.170
make it our own ranch, and
we'll create our own feedlot.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:02.310
GABE WEISS: And you had
never ranched before?

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:02.640
RON HICKS: Never.

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:03.900
GABE WEISS: So this
was totally fresh.

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:04.775
I'm going to come in.

00:16:04.775 --> 00:16:06.310
Ah, I'm just going
to make a ranch.

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:07.660
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
How hard could it be?

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:08.400
GABE WEISS: Hard.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:09.340
RON HICKS: It was
harder than we thought.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:11.010
But I think the
issue is we realized

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:13.010
if we do this in a way
in which we would call it

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:15.870
the most humane way of raising
the animal, using natural means

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.790
and bringing in
these tech systems

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:20.910
that we were just going to place
out there and start to test,

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:23.100
even the rancher herself
said, I thought you were

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:24.690
in a different
world, and said, I

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:26.820
think this is something that
I'd like to actually participate

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:27.210
in.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:28.793
And if you remember,
you met with her.

00:16:28.793 --> 00:16:29.310
Sure.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:29.943
GABE WEISS: She was
the one sitting with us

00:16:29.943 --> 00:16:30.510
at the luncheon.

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:30.930
RON HICKS: At the luncheon.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:31.888
GABE WEISS: Yeah, yeah.

00:16:31.888 --> 00:16:32.770
Almost a year ago--

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:34.020
almost exactly a year ago now.

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:34.653
RON HICKS: Yep.

00:16:34.653 --> 00:16:36.570
And she was proud of the
actual output of what

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:39.780
we could call a high-performance
animal-- never needed medicine,

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:43.290
gained weight beautifully, and
was absolutely incredible--

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:44.790
the flavor, everything about it.

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:45.560
GABE WEISS: I'm remembering.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:46.200
RON HICKS: And remember this.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.700
I mean, we're talking about the
middle of a bell curve here.

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:52.120
That's the primary beef in
this country is feedlot raised.

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:55.320
So we changed the
basis of feedlots

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:57.450
with someone that'd
never seen it before.

00:16:57.450 --> 00:16:59.490
And she ends up
becoming a superstar--

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:01.170
sold everything immediately.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:02.464
All the animals were sold.

00:17:02.464 --> 00:17:05.089
So she's in the practice now of
actually being one of the top--

00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:06.750
what I would call--
high-tech ranchers, just

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:08.605
by the sheer fact that
we drove by one day

00:17:08.605 --> 00:17:09.480
and leased her place.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:10.230
GABE WEISS: That's awesome.

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:10.734
I love that.

00:17:10.734 --> 00:17:12.359
You told me a story
at one point, which

00:17:12.359 --> 00:17:13.984
I would love for you
to regale us with,

00:17:13.984 --> 00:17:18.545
of when you were kind of testing
the AI system against humans.

00:17:18.545 --> 00:17:20.670
It was like master's students
in veterinary system,

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:21.140
and you wanted to--

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:21.599
RON HICKS: Absolutely, yeah.

00:17:21.599 --> 00:17:23.130
GABE WEISS: --prove that
your system could predict

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:24.297
as accurately as they could.

00:17:24.297 --> 00:17:26.130
RON HICKS: Austin's
talking about anomalies,

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:27.839
or what we call
something that's odd.

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:30.214
And I think that you guys here
at Google understand this.

00:17:30.214 --> 00:17:33.150
If you can take in enough
data, you can really see a lot.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:36.390
But if you go into an area
that's not ever been watched,

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:38.940
you can surprise some of
the best in the world.

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:42.150
And we went out to meet with
some of the high-end vets that

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:43.482
actually do feedlot management.

00:17:43.482 --> 00:17:45.440
And they said, Ron, we
have a graduating class,

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:48.690
and in the summer, we always
make the graduates work

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:50.530
through the final year.

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:52.350
And we do stuff like test them.

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:54.880
Can we test you
with our graduates?

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.648
We are the best,
and these graduates

00:17:56.648 --> 00:17:58.440
are what we consider
to be some of the top,

00:17:58.440 --> 00:17:59.910
so they've made it
through the program.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:00.930
Bring your stuff out.

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:03.472
Let's watch some animals, and
you let us know what you think.

00:18:03.472 --> 00:18:05.010
We literally set
the antennas up.

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:06.360
No one told us anything.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.500
We drove away.

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:10.050
We were probably 500
yards from the site.

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:12.090
And we could see from
our system the animal

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:13.913
that was, without a
doubt, in trouble.

00:18:13.913 --> 00:18:15.080
GABE WEISS: The system knew.

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:15.870
RON HICKS: The system knew.

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:16.260
GABE WEISS: Yeah.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:17.460
RON HICKS: We were
actually embarrassed

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:18.830
because we knew so quickly.

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:21.330
So our chief engineer said, we
need to go back and tell him.

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:23.596
I said, we need to
wait about 30 minutes.

00:18:23.596 --> 00:18:24.668
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:24.668 --> 00:18:26.460
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
you said that you knew

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:27.845
within a minute or seconds--

00:18:27.845 --> 00:18:28.180
RON HICKS: We knew.

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:28.590
Oh, yeah.

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:29.370
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
You're just like, oh,

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:31.020
that's the problem
one, but we can't

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:32.370
go back and embarrass them.

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:34.870
RON HICKS: We were sitting
in a Suburban up the road.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:36.495
We didn't want to
turn around because I

00:18:36.495 --> 00:18:39.192
was embarrassed myself
for the poor students, who

00:18:39.192 --> 00:18:40.650
were out there with
their notebooks

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:43.170
and trying to look almost
like a pen rider would

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:45.030
because that's a very
strong point here.

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:47.510
A pen rider can give
you one viewpoint.

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:49.260
But if you look at
data on a general basis

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.595
and it's recalculating
and reformatting,

00:18:51.595 --> 00:18:53.970
it doesn't take long for us
to see what comes to the top.

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:54.910
And we did it.

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:56.160
GABE WEISS: So did you just
go drive in circles for a bit

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:56.790
before you came back?

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:57.070
RON HICKS: We did.

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:57.390
GABE WEISS: Yeah.

00:18:57.390 --> 00:18:58.140
RON HICKS: We did.

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:00.590
We picked up some barbecue
at a little place here.

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:02.770
And again, I'm not a
big ranch type person,

00:19:02.770 --> 00:19:06.300
so walked in, got some barbecue,
stalled, went back to the lot,

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:08.250
told the vets, we
know the animal.

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:09.750
And we obviously put it on
a little piece of paper,

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:10.320
handed it to him.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:10.865
He's shocked.

00:19:10.865 --> 00:19:13.170
And he's trying to slow it down,
but he said to those students,

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:13.950
let's come in.

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:15.360
They turned in their results.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:16.325
We turned in ours.

00:19:16.325 --> 00:19:17.700
We were the only
correct answer--

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:18.900
GABE WEISS: Hm.

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:19.380
RON HICKS: --the only one.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:20.350
GABE WEISS: That's
unfortunate for them.

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:21.660
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
But it's hard, right?

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.310
That is the reality
of this situation,

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:25.633
is that it can be really
difficult to observe

00:19:25.633 --> 00:19:26.550
these sorts of things.

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:28.890
And being able to collect,
like you said earlier,

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:31.728
tremendous amounts of data and
then make predictions on it

00:19:31.728 --> 00:19:33.520
is something that just
hasn't been possible

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:34.520
up until recently.

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:35.520
RON HICKS: That's right.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:37.140
GABE WEISS: I mean, these
pen riders have the lifetime

00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:38.277
of doing the job, right?

00:19:38.277 --> 00:19:40.110
So they've got just a
lifetime of experience

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:41.560
of gathering that data.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:45.090
So in essence, they're kind of
the hard drives or the storage,

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:46.430
whatever you want to call it.

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:48.555
When one of them retires,
all that tribal knowledge

00:19:48.555 --> 00:19:49.790
is [WHOOSH] gone.

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:53.160
It's so cool to me that there
is no leaving the system.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:54.050
The data is there.

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:54.800
The data is there.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:56.730
So it's really more
of a factor of,

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:58.230
this can just be
applied everywhere.

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:00.480
There's no reason not to
just apply this all over.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:02.430
AUSTIN ADAMS: It's way
more possible to export

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:05.325
a model from AI than it
would be from a person.

00:20:05.325 --> 00:20:07.200
GABE WEISS: Human
knowledge is tough to dump.

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:08.520
Anyone that's left a job knows.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.410
I've got two weeks to
dump all of my knowledge.

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:11.120
That's not a thing.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:11.610
How you do that?

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:12.330
You can't do that.

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:13.770
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And if you're
looking for another business

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:16.830
idea, finding a way to
export human-trained models

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:18.190
would be fascinating.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:19.010
RON HICKS: So on
that, obviously, we--

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:20.843
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh,
are you working on it?

00:20:20.843 --> 00:20:22.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.370
RON HICKS: We can't discount
the years of experience

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:27.150
that the pen riders that
do still work in feedlots.

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:31.500
So we have built features
into our ecosystem,

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:34.590
apps, whatever you call them,
that do allow human feedback.

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:37.980
So we'll make what we call a
prediction or recommendation.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:41.760
But we still allow and track the
negation or acceptance of that

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:42.823
from an actual pen rider.

00:20:42.823 --> 00:20:44.490
GABE WEISS: I'm
guessing that then, that

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:45.770
gets folded into the model.

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:47.560
RON HICKS: As a lot of people
are aware, machine learning,

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:48.600
you have to put
weights on things.

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:50.400
This big weighted
average machine's

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:52.463
just churning at high speed.

00:20:52.463 --> 00:20:53.130
GABE WEISS: Yep.

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:54.930
RON HICKS: So you
put a specific weight

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:57.760
on that, a feature
or that data point,

00:20:57.760 --> 00:20:59.010
and it can get folded in.

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:01.080
So it's a function
of understanding

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.000
how important the
different variables are

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.270
in different cases and then
making good predictions that

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:09.330
are not noisy, they're
not false positives,

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:11.700
and they're also
manageable by the staff.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:13.770
Because when you go into
a feedlot that might

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:15.600
be really not doing so great--

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:16.860
MARK MIRCHANDANI: You need
a lot more antibiotics.

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:18.360
RON HICKS: Maybe,
or in the sense

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:21.630
of being able to provide
relevant and manageable

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:22.228
predictions.

00:21:22.228 --> 00:21:24.270
GABE WEISS: So we have
this concept of greenfield

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:25.110
versus brownfield.

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:27.330
The idea is, does
your system work?

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:29.340
You mentioned a feedlot
that's in bad shape

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:31.680
and lots of sick animals
or whatever it is.

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:34.080
Can the system be deployed
and fix brownfield

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:36.877
so you could take a feedlot like
that that's in really bad shape

00:21:36.877 --> 00:21:38.460
and be like, hey,
bring in our system.

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:39.710
We'll help fix your situation.

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:40.350
Can it do that?

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:40.860
RON HICKS: Oh, yeah.

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:41.160
Yeah.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:42.577
AUSTIN ADAMS: We
think that if you

00:21:42.577 --> 00:21:44.730
deploy our system, our
monitoring, in the right way

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:46.530
on a feedlot like
that, if you have

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:49.440
a good strategy for treating
those animals, which we have

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:52.170
a couple of other technologies
that can help you with that,

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:54.555
we think you could
turn that place around.

00:21:54.555 --> 00:21:57.360
And I think there's evidence
of that in some past research

00:21:57.360 --> 00:21:59.565
that Ron and people
before me have done, yeah.

00:21:59.565 --> 00:22:01.440
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And
that's kind of the idea

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:04.620
here is not to completely
automate the entire farm,

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:05.400
right?

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:06.640
Or the ranch, in this case.

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:09.300
The idea here is
to actually gather

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:11.730
all of this data, which
wasn't possible before, make

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:13.860
predictions on this
data, which wasn't really

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:16.700
possible before minus
human intuition, which is

00:22:16.700 --> 00:22:18.077
an astoundingly powerful thing.

00:22:18.077 --> 00:22:19.910
But like you said, it's
difficult to export.

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:22.200
It's difficult to transfer
that to different people.

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:24.830
But it's also limited in
terms of what one person who

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:26.363
comes in and sees it is.

00:22:26.363 --> 00:22:28.280
So with your system, you
collect all the data,

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:30.020
you make the prediction, and
then you give it to the humans,

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:32.330
and they can implement
changes based on that, right?

00:22:32.330 --> 00:22:35.070
Like a lot of AI, it's not
trying to replace humans.

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:37.040
It's trying to make
them much more efficient

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:40.100
at not only getting, but also
predicting, based off of data

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:41.810
that just wasn't possible.

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:43.977
RON HICKS: Yeah, I mean,
if you think about the fact

00:22:43.977 --> 00:22:46.280
that we showed an early
tool to a university that's

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:48.760
great at what they do,
that tool becomes better.

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:50.810
And as soon as the
input is, I think,

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:53.480
shared across a number
of users, it even

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:55.340
improves through
the human interface.

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:56.570
GABE WEISS: It's artificial
intelligence, right?

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:56.645
RON HICKS: Absolutely.

00:22:56.645 --> 00:22:58.230
GABE WEISS: So the idea is
the more inputs you get,

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:00.480
the better the system gets,
the more accurate it gets.

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:02.068
So broader adoption
just means it's

00:23:02.068 --> 00:23:03.860
going to be better and
better at predicting

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:06.460
the anomalies of an animal
that's not behaving properly.

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:07.970
It's just going to get
better at doing that.

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:08.750
RON HICKS: And
this is something I

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:10.670
think we should maybe
talk a little bit about,

00:23:10.670 --> 00:23:11.990
the whole global impact.

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:14.210
If you can imagine now,
converging information

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:15.770
from different
parts of the world,

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:17.900
looking at different
animal movement criteria,

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:20.090
you're talking about
global perspectives here.

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:23.270
And I think we can automate
a really nice system based

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:24.800
on the fact that
there is a desire

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:27.140
to have animals that
are high performance,

00:23:27.140 --> 00:23:29.480
clean, and retailed
properly because we're

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:32.190
bringing the transparency
now to the retailer.

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:34.670
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And I
imagine that these models have

00:23:34.670 --> 00:23:36.770
a lot in common,
but how different

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:38.840
is that data from
a ranch in Texas

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:40.740
versus a ranch in,
let's say, Australia?

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:41.990
RON HICKS: So the difference--

00:23:41.990 --> 00:23:43.448
I mean, Austin
could go into this--

00:23:43.448 --> 00:23:46.010
but we obviously have
environmental changes,

00:23:46.010 --> 00:23:48.740
and obviously, the
breed, the genetics.

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:50.780
Surprisingly, the
fact that movement

00:23:50.780 --> 00:23:53.210
is so vital to an
animal, especially

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:55.867
if they're in a stressed
condition, is somewhat similar,

00:23:55.867 --> 00:23:58.200
but you've got all the other
pieces that come into play.

00:23:58.200 --> 00:23:59.060
GABE WEISS: So a
stress situation

00:23:59.060 --> 00:24:01.700
might look very different
in a hot, humid environment

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:04.437
like Texas versus,
say, an arid situation.

00:24:04.437 --> 00:24:05.270
RON HICKS: Oh, yeah.

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:07.460
AUSTIN ADAMS: Day
one, implementing,

00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:09.710
Australia and somewhere
in Wyoming, whatever,

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:12.020
totally different
temperature, we can still

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:15.080
predict the animals that are
sick just on the way they move.

00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:17.430
The goal is to get better
and better at that,

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:18.680
adding in the other variables.

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:20.780
GABE WEISS: And better and
better translates to how soon--

00:24:20.780 --> 00:24:21.290
how fast?

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:22.665
AUSTIN ADAMS:
Better and better--

00:24:22.665 --> 00:24:26.480
how fast, also being able to
filter out any noise that we

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:28.500
might be creating
based on other factors,

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:31.430
such as a different
terrain, blah, blah, blah,

00:24:31.430 --> 00:24:34.610
things like that, or
heat, humidity levels,

00:24:34.610 --> 00:24:37.020
the moisture content of
the grass or their food,

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:38.280
different things like that.

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:40.110
So those are some
of those variables.

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:43.190
But given the difference
of hours or days

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:47.495
on their behavior, we can still
predict which ones are sick.

00:24:47.495 --> 00:24:50.082
And that's been proven through
different temperature scenarios

00:24:50.082 --> 00:24:52.040
and things like that,
that, again, the research

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:53.540
that early HerdX did.

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:56.870
So a lot of what's happened
in HerdX is amazing technology

00:24:56.870 --> 00:24:58.670
and ideas have been
developed, and now

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:01.790
the phase that we're in now is
actually taking that, realizing

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:04.550
the data value of it,
bringing it into the cloud,

00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:05.870
and scaling that piece.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:08.210
So a lot of this cool
stuff has actually

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:10.618
been implemented on farms
and things like that.

00:25:10.618 --> 00:25:12.410
And what we're now
doing is packaging it up

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:15.830
where it can be run in a
modern way and on the cloud,

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:17.577
and scale out to the
entire globe, too.

00:25:17.577 --> 00:25:19.660
GABE WEISS: Yeah, we call
that the lift and shift.

00:25:19.660 --> 00:25:20.300
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah.

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:20.930
GABE WEISS: Once
you've got it all

00:25:20.930 --> 00:25:22.573
set up in your
local environment,

00:25:22.573 --> 00:25:23.990
and you have it
all humming along,

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:26.180
now it's time to get rid of
the infrastructure requirements

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:27.305
and just get it out, right?

00:25:27.305 --> 00:25:28.680
So I know that
we've been talking

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:30.600
a lot about the first
part of this business.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:31.790
But I know there's more.

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:34.100
You talked about
originally the transparency

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:35.760
all the way to the consumer.

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.850
So let's talk a little
bit about what that means.

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:38.890
We've now got a
good idea of what

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:40.015
it looks like on the ranch.

00:25:40.015 --> 00:25:42.750
So we've got the livestock's
all being taken care of.

00:25:42.750 --> 00:25:45.637
They're getting improved health
conditions on the feedlots.

00:25:45.637 --> 00:25:46.970
How does this help the consumer?

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:49.670
Where does the consumer come
into this story for HerdX?

00:25:49.670 --> 00:25:50.870
RON HICKS: Austin and I
could both jump into this.

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:51.620
It's kind of fun.

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:54.260
We've been watching this happen
over the last year for him,

00:25:54.260 --> 00:25:56.090
and the last five years for me.

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:59.270
The retail experience
is radically different

00:25:59.270 --> 00:26:00.560
than it was five years ago.

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:04.640
The amount of data that's
necessary to make a purchase

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:07.720
has to be high speed
and has to be relevant.

00:26:07.720 --> 00:26:12.320
So your buyer is more aware,
in certain categories of food,

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:14.330
as to what they're eating
and how it was raised.

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:17.420
In the case of US beef,
that's our goal now,

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:19.400
is to really bring
that transparency

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:20.925
all the way through a--

00:26:20.925 --> 00:26:22.550
we'll call it a
blockchain perspective,

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:24.500
but it's the methodology
of participants

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:28.700
that all work to make sure
that the food is high end,

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:31.040
treated in a more natural
way in the beginning,

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:33.320
and then processed in
the best conditions,

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:35.570
and then moved and trucked
under the right conditions.

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:37.280
By putting all those
pieces together,

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:40.125
you've created a really
awesome system for retailers.

00:26:40.125 --> 00:26:42.500
GABE WEISS: It feels like--
and correct me if I'm wrong--

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:43.877
the concept of
the farm-to-table.

00:26:43.877 --> 00:26:46.460
That's what we're talking about,
is people are really enamored

00:26:46.460 --> 00:26:49.010
of the farm-to-table concept
of knowing this food came

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:50.527
from the farm a mile away.

00:26:50.527 --> 00:26:52.610
So it sounds like what
you're kind of, in essence,

00:26:52.610 --> 00:26:55.550
doing is extending the
reach, the range, of what

00:26:55.550 --> 00:26:56.702
that farm-to-table means.

00:26:56.702 --> 00:26:58.910
The food doesn't have to be
next door to you anymore.

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:01.250
It can be at a ranch
hundreds of miles away.

00:27:01.250 --> 00:27:05.120
But you're giving the ability
to see, for those end consumers,

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:07.520
what the origin point looks
like and where it's from.

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:08.562
What does that look like?

00:27:08.562 --> 00:27:10.790
For instance, I'm going to
go buy some meat, right?

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:12.140
RON HICKS: Yep, and let's
do this in a way in which--

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:13.848
I think Austin can
help break this down--

00:27:13.848 --> 00:27:16.880
from a market perspective, or
maybe the niche that HerdX has.

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:19.580
I think there's a given on
anything that's pasture raised,

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:21.450
that's out there just
running and roaming,

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:22.340
it's a good animal.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:24.500
It has not been
removed from its mom.

00:27:24.500 --> 00:27:26.750
It's running out at a
really well-run pasture.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:29.510
So the given is it's safe.

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:30.350
It's clean.

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:31.202
It's a great animal.

00:27:31.202 --> 00:27:31.910
High performance.

00:27:31.910 --> 00:27:34.310
What I'm saying that it's a
high performance end good.

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:36.350
In the case of the feedlot,
again, the majority

00:27:36.350 --> 00:27:38.060
of the entire US business--

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:40.700
24 million animals on feed
here in the US today--

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:42.343
how do you take care of those?

00:27:42.343 --> 00:27:44.010
So that's what this
system is all about.

00:27:44.010 --> 00:27:45.950
And I think the
advantage is it's

00:27:45.950 --> 00:27:49.160
the majority of this
country's livestock and beef

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:50.660
that's raised on a feedlot.

00:27:50.660 --> 00:27:52.830
That is not a
pasture--raised animal.

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:55.370
And that's what we're basically
putting a mechanism in place

00:27:55.370 --> 00:27:59.210
to allow that to be the future
of how beef will be raised

00:27:59.210 --> 00:28:01.240
and how it's made
available to the retailer.

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:04.280
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah, so we
analyzed the cattle supply

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:06.680
chain, the meat supply
chain, and looking

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:08.480
at the different
countries involved.

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:11.090
And they all want
a tracing program,

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:12.830
and there's so many
hurdles to that,

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:15.350
and people who don't want
to share data with who,

00:28:15.350 --> 00:28:18.060
or people who are kind of on
the fringe of the supply chain.

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:22.700
So we selected the technology
and the methodology

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:26.930
of just having each
person just have a way

00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:30.128
to manage the transaction
on the batch of animals.

00:28:30.128 --> 00:28:31.670
Although we can with
the data-- we're

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:34.670
not trying to be one of those
people that shows you the nose

00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:36.260
print of the cow
that you're eating--

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:39.740
we don't think that the
consumer really is into that.

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:40.010
GABE WEISS: That
might be a bit much.

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:40.740
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah.

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:42.020
I think what the
consumer wants is

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:43.520
a connection with
another person, not

00:28:43.520 --> 00:28:44.430
a connection with an animal.

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:45.680
GABE WEISS: The rancher, yeah.

00:28:45.680 --> 00:28:48.020
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah, so we're
trying to basically lift

00:28:48.020 --> 00:28:50.515
the rancher, the producer
where the animal has been most

00:28:50.515 --> 00:28:51.890
of its life,
because they're only

00:28:51.890 --> 00:28:54.600
at a feedlot for maybe three
months, maybe five months.

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:56.600
So the majority of
its life, it was

00:28:56.600 --> 00:29:01.010
raised as a great animal on
a pasture in some small town

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:03.380
with some just super
quality people.

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:05.535
And then it gets moved
into the supply chain

00:29:05.535 --> 00:29:06.410
where it gets hidden.

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:09.200
And those people, they're
having to fight for business

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:09.990
all the time.

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:12.800
So we're trying to
highlight those people,

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:16.140
create more incentive for people
to have longer term contracts

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:18.140
with each other because
they're working together

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:21.110
on a supply chain, which
ends up making them all more

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:22.730
profitable, while
at the same time,

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:25.220
not changing the middle
of the bell curve of meat.

00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:27.680
Because we want basically the
pasture-raised farm-to-table

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:28.790
for every family.

00:29:28.790 --> 00:29:30.020
We want that for everyone.

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:32.840
So how we do it is we outfit
the different supply chain

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:34.807
partners, or we integrate
with technology.

00:29:34.807 --> 00:29:36.390
So we integrate with
their technology,

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:38.120
or we outfit them
with technology.

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:41.060
And they're able to
check in, check out

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:43.510
at that transaction time, at
the moment of transaction.

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:46.760
Where the animal or the
product is moving hands,

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:50.090
we're able to take their paper
processes, make them digital.

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:53.498
But then because it's going
into a distributed ledger--

00:29:53.498 --> 00:29:55.040
because I hate that
word blockchain--

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:58.610
it's something that we're trying
to build for 50 years to come.

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:00.620
We're trying to select
a technology that's

00:30:00.620 --> 00:30:04.220
future-ish proof, something
that can be managed

00:30:04.220 --> 00:30:05.990
by the industry moving forward.

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:08.190
That's the reason for the
distributed nature of it.

00:30:08.190 --> 00:30:10.620
It's not because blockchain
is the hot thing.

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:12.770
It's because that actually
makes sense in this case

00:30:12.770 --> 00:30:16.280
because they will start to
manage the industry long

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:17.810
after maybe HerdX--

00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:20.030
in 20 years, maybe
HerdX isn't a thing.

00:30:20.030 --> 00:30:20.580
Who knows?

00:30:20.580 --> 00:30:23.180
But the cattle industry
all over the world

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:25.430
needs a distributed
method for having

00:30:25.430 --> 00:30:26.890
a single source of truth.

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:27.640
GABE WEISS: Right.

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:29.848
AUSTIN ADAMS: Because that's
what the consumers need,

00:30:29.848 --> 00:30:32.510
and that's also what agencies
need, health agencies need,

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:34.310
people need in recall scenarios.

00:30:34.310 --> 00:30:35.270
There's a lot of--

00:30:35.270 --> 00:30:37.340
what's the opposite
of collateral damage--

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:37.730
GABE WEISS: [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:38.938
AUSTIN ADAMS: --that happens?

00:30:38.938 --> 00:30:39.620
Yeah.

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:42.290
No, I mean,
collateral improvement

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:46.727
when you get a system that can
know the source ranch and all

00:30:46.727 --> 00:30:48.560
the steps in between
that something's taken.

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:51.000
Because it's not the
producer that has a problem.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:53.580
It's many steps down the road
and many changes of hands.

00:30:53.580 --> 00:30:55.560
It could even be
in a bad scenario.

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:58.370
Obviously, HerdX's
technology is focused

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:00.707
on removing the bad
animal before they

00:31:00.707 --> 00:31:01.790
get into the supply chain.

00:31:01.790 --> 00:31:03.690
That's a big, huge goal.

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:06.890
But there's also the
ability to detect the issues

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:09.770
within the supply chain due to
timing, temperature, control,

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:10.700
things like that.

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:12.825
Because a lot of times,
it's just a package of meat

00:31:12.825 --> 00:31:15.555
sitting on an airport because a
truck is late, kind of a thing.

00:31:15.555 --> 00:31:17.180
That's where sometimes
things go wrong,

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:19.380
and people get sick
and stuff like that.

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:21.860
And so what we're
hoping to do is, first

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:24.410
off, remove the bad
animal at the beginning.

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:27.140
Because when you have a
higher quality animal,

00:31:27.140 --> 00:31:29.510
the shelf life of that
product is actually better

00:31:29.510 --> 00:31:31.700
because of the stuff
inside the meat.

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:34.730
But then when it gets
into the supply chain,

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:37.280
taking away the
hidden tactics that

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:39.005
make that a little less safe.

00:31:39.005 --> 00:31:39.880
Does that make sense?

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:39.970
GABE WEISS: Yeah.

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:42.920
It's being able to backtrack if
there is a bad actor somewhere

00:31:42.920 --> 00:31:44.193
along the supply chain.

00:31:44.193 --> 00:31:45.860
Like you said, it
might be a pallet that

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:47.463
was left for a
little bit too long,

00:31:47.463 --> 00:31:49.130
and now everything
in that pallet's bad,

00:31:49.130 --> 00:31:50.588
but everything in
that pallet might

00:31:50.588 --> 00:31:53.240
be forked all over the place
from different ranchers.

00:31:53.240 --> 00:31:56.630
If the ledger can identify
from all of the same meat

00:31:56.630 --> 00:31:58.840
back to one bottleneck
point, you now

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:01.580
know that you found
your bad actor.

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:04.610
So the core of the health
system of watching over

00:32:04.610 --> 00:32:07.152
these animals, that all
happens at the feedlot level.

00:32:07.152 --> 00:32:09.110
But you're saying this
ledger-- the blockchain,

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:10.777
for lack of a better
way of putting it--

00:32:10.777 --> 00:32:12.950
the receipt of transfer
extends all the way back

00:32:12.950 --> 00:32:15.515
to the initial ranchers,
the raising of the animals.

00:32:15.515 --> 00:32:17.640
So it's like raising of
the animals to the feedlots

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:18.390
to the packing in.

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:20.440
So you're tracking
the whole chain.

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:21.440
How far down does it go?

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:23.330
Does it go all the way to the
end consumers, the stores?

00:32:23.330 --> 00:32:24.320
How far does it go?

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:26.600
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah, so
think of it backward.

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:28.580
So we're working
as hard as we can

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:31.610
to get as close to birth
of the animal as we can.

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:33.890
The easier parts are
starting at the grocery store

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:34.850
and working backward.

00:32:34.850 --> 00:32:36.563
So not that it's
a huge challenge.

00:32:36.563 --> 00:32:39.230
I'm saying the harder part is to
get propagated all the way back

00:32:39.230 --> 00:32:43.590
to these ranches that are on
2G basically kind of a thing.

00:32:43.590 --> 00:32:45.470
So that's where we
love our business model

00:32:45.470 --> 00:32:47.060
because it pulls us
into those places

00:32:47.060 --> 00:32:48.602
so we don't have to
go look for them.

00:32:48.602 --> 00:32:52.230
They're just, we get an address
basically kind of a thing.

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:54.230
But it does go all the
way to the grocery store,

00:32:54.230 --> 00:32:55.730
and that's really
the consumer side.

00:32:55.730 --> 00:32:56.690
Go ahead, Ron.

00:32:56.690 --> 00:32:59.000
RON HICKS: Yeah, the
grocery is needing

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:00.822
to make sure they
retain customers.

00:33:00.822 --> 00:33:03.280
And so if you think about it,
if you can actually highlight

00:33:03.280 --> 00:33:05.360
a new meet product,
have a storyboard back

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:07.970
to a hot producer, you've
completed the chain,

00:33:07.970 --> 00:33:10.480
but in a sense, you've
improved retail.

00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:12.740
And so I think there's
a huge advantage.

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:14.870
Again, in our case,
we recognize that when

00:33:14.870 --> 00:33:17.403
an animal comes through it's
divided into many parts.

00:33:17.403 --> 00:33:19.570
And so you've got some that
go to a restaurant, some

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:23.060
that goes to a grocer, some
will be ground into ground beef.

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:26.227
And so the advantage for us
is providing that transparency

00:33:26.227 --> 00:33:27.060
all the way through.

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:29.600
We believe that's the actual
retail thrust of our company.

00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:30.920
GABE WEISS: Are
you matching people

00:33:30.920 --> 00:33:32.450
doing A/B testing with
different ranchers?

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:33.810
And they're like, oh, no,
the meat from this rancher

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:34.810
is really where it's at.

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:36.893
RON HICKS: So it's interesting
you're saying that.

00:33:36.893 --> 00:33:38.460
I think it's a new
market advantage.

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:40.430
And I'm sure, if some
are listening today,

00:33:40.430 --> 00:33:43.940
that the retailer is always
looking for something, right?

00:33:43.940 --> 00:33:46.130
Especially the big market
managers or the product

00:33:46.130 --> 00:33:46.880
managers.

00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:49.880
If you can imagine, if we
go back to the original test

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:52.670
ranch of the many that I've
had, she sold all of her meat

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:53.580
immediately.

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:56.240
So if you really ate at
that restaurant that night--

00:33:56.240 --> 00:33:57.885
and I think Gabe,
you were with us--

00:33:57.885 --> 00:34:00.260
if you think about a grocer
up the street, a small grocer

00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:02.900
here in Austin that
carries that same brand,

00:34:02.900 --> 00:34:04.760
they may ask for hers again.

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:06.870
Think about that on the
backside coming back.

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:09.889
So now we're providing a report
back to the producer, who

00:34:09.889 --> 00:34:12.380
says we need to have so
many more of your animal,

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:16.190
so much weight, so many cuts,
to come to this Austin grocer

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:18.710
due to the restaurant
that was just up the road.

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:19.730
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well,
it's like you said.

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:22.130
This is visible throughout
the entire supply chain and--

00:34:22.130 --> 00:34:22.520
RON HICKS: Absolutely.

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:23.270
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
--at each step,

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:25.429
they're going to look back
and say, I want this one

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:26.989
because this one has more data.

00:34:26.989 --> 00:34:28.080
I know this is good.

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:29.622
And here's the
reasons why it's good.

00:34:29.622 --> 00:34:32.063
I now have observability in
ways that I didn't before.

00:34:32.063 --> 00:34:33.980
And that goes from all
the way from the ranch,

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.500
up, up, up, all the way to the
person who goes and buys it,

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:38.989
whether that's a restaurant,
whether that's just a person

00:34:38.989 --> 00:34:40.080
going to a grocery store.

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:41.288
RON HICKS: Yeah, the thrill--

00:34:41.288 --> 00:34:44.600
I literally-- even today,
our work with you guys,

00:34:44.600 --> 00:34:45.719
we could do this globally.

00:34:45.719 --> 00:34:46.636
So I'm in New Zealand.

00:34:46.636 --> 00:34:47.900
I leave for Japan on Monday.

00:34:47.900 --> 00:34:50.000
I'm in New Zealand
the following week.

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:53.270
We're providing the same
reports back to them of requests

00:34:53.270 --> 00:34:56.840
for those animals that has
never been seen before.

00:34:56.840 --> 00:35:00.290
We're providing to the
retailer reports of information

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:01.400
they've never seen before.

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:03.930
We're marrying, basically,
those two ends of the bookends.

00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:04.940
GABE WEISS: And I'm
assuming people are super

00:35:04.940 --> 00:35:06.023
