eye-opening for these folks

00:35:08.630 --> 00:35:10.898
that's like, I can know what?

00:35:10.898 --> 00:35:11.690
RON HICKS: Exactly.

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:12.970
GABE WEISS: How?

00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:13.970
That's the magic, right?

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:14.770
RON HICKS: That's it.

00:35:14.770 --> 00:35:16.280
GABE WEISS: That's
hopefully what's offered.

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:18.530
AUSTIN ADAMS: One thing we
talk about a lot with HerdX

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:20.960
is HerdX isn't the only
one that could implement

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:22.188
a traceability program.

00:35:22.188 --> 00:35:24.230
There's a lot of people
that are trying to do it.

00:35:24.230 --> 00:35:26.427
The USDA has been
trying for how long?

00:35:26.427 --> 00:35:27.260
RON HICKS: 21 years.

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:28.218
AUSTIN ADAMS: 21 years.

00:35:28.218 --> 00:35:30.500
We love the USDA, and
we think they can do it.

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:32.180
And we are here to help.

00:35:32.180 --> 00:35:33.680
But there's something
different when

00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:35.450
you have a supply
chain that's also

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:38.360
got what we call
authenticated data, meaning

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:41.210
data that's collected
without human intervention

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:42.410
or interaction.

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:45.290
So we have automatic
monitoring systems

00:35:45.290 --> 00:35:47.948
that can verify the source
country of the animal.

00:35:47.948 --> 00:35:49.740
It can verify a number
of different things.

00:35:49.740 --> 00:35:53.180
It can actually also
play into however

00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:55.700
far we want to move
the movement data,

00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:58.760
even over to the retailer to
give them a stamp of assurance

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:01.880
or some form of quality
metrics on this animal.

00:36:01.880 --> 00:36:03.927
When you think about
higher quality retailers,

00:36:03.927 --> 00:36:05.510
or when you think
about retailers that

00:36:05.510 --> 00:36:07.700
are just super risk averse--

00:36:07.700 --> 00:36:09.440
I'm thinking of a
name right now--

00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:13.280
they want as much data as they
can on mitigating their risk.

00:36:13.280 --> 00:36:16.160
So they want the highest
quality, lowest cost animals.

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:19.590
So the data that we're able
to provide, but not just data,

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:21.050
but authenticated
data, data that

00:36:21.050 --> 00:36:24.050
isn't just someone scanning
a piece of paper, which

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:25.580
could be fraught with error.

00:36:25.580 --> 00:36:28.550
It's authenticated data from
automatic monitoring systems

00:36:28.550 --> 00:36:31.130
that we actually know
if someone's opened

00:36:31.130 --> 00:36:32.660
the panel on our hardware.

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:35.120
So it's like we can
put different weights

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:35.960
on different data.

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:38.840
So we think that
authenticated data or thinking

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:41.570
about data in a way that's
like, hey, this is not just

00:36:41.570 --> 00:36:42.740
manual data digitized.

00:36:42.740 --> 00:36:45.330
This is a new form of data
that didn't exist before.

00:36:45.330 --> 00:36:47.960
And it's collected in
an autonomous way, which

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.660
I think heightens your ability
to trust it because there's

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:51.840
no human intervention.

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:53.610
There's no bad actors
available in there.

00:36:53.610 --> 00:36:54.590
GABE WEISS: It's not
subjective anymore.

00:36:54.590 --> 00:36:55.650
AUSTIN ADAMS: Yeah,
it's not subjective.

00:36:55.650 --> 00:36:58.192
GABE WEISS: So this brings up
kind of an interesting thought,

00:36:58.192 --> 00:37:00.660
which is that the USDA offers
