00:37:23.862 --> 00:37:25.820
AUSTIN ADAMS: So I think
some of those agencies

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:30.390
aren't necessarily aware of
what this sort of ledger,

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:32.330
this sort of network can do.

00:37:32.330 --> 00:37:33.650
GABE WEISS: They haven't put
two and two together yet.

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:34.730
AUSTIN ADAMS: And
think about the fact

00:37:34.730 --> 00:37:35.980
that it could be co-operative.

00:37:35.980 --> 00:37:37.940
It couldn't be as bad
as what you're saying.

00:37:37.940 --> 00:37:39.860
It can be a cooperative
thing with them.

00:37:39.860 --> 00:37:41.870
That's part of the
goal of the network,

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:44.150
again, is this is
for the industry.

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:45.510
Obviously, we're a company.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:46.670
We have to make money.

00:37:46.670 --> 00:37:48.380
But this is for the industry.

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:51.480
And you think about the way
that blockchains can work.

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:55.190
We have our smart contract that
manages the change of hands

00:37:55.190 --> 00:37:56.300
on the network.

00:37:56.300 --> 00:37:58.550
But there's other contracts
that can be written

00:37:58.550 --> 00:38:00.080
on top of the same network.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:04.100
So the organic certification
could be a function of data in,

00:38:04.100 --> 00:38:06.470
certification out on a
specific batch of meat.

00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:09.140
So those are some of the things
that maybe you still have

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:10.370
an overhead cost or whatever.

00:38:10.370 --> 00:38:12.200
This is just ideation.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:14.780
Those are some things that
you can do with-- weird thing

00:38:14.780 --> 00:38:17.760
to say-- a data
network, a ledger.

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:20.090
So how that works
out is you have

00:38:20.090 --> 00:38:21.828
third parties and
agencies involved

00:38:21.828 --> 00:38:23.120
in different parts of the data.

00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:25.618
Because, again, the beauty
