of mechanical
servo or something,

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:08.662
and I want it to respond
to an event in the cloud--

00:43:08.662 --> 00:43:10.870
like, let's say if somebody
uploads a file or someone

00:43:10.870 --> 00:43:13.000
changes some parameter
and that causes a trigger,

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:14.333
what does it actually look like?

00:43:14.333 --> 00:43:15.950
Those are two very
separate concepts.

00:43:15.950 --> 00:43:17.317
So how does that work?

00:43:17.317 --> 00:43:18.650
GABE WEISS: So it's interesting.

00:43:18.650 --> 00:43:20.630
It's not as hard
as you might think.

00:43:20.630 --> 00:43:24.250
So we've got a product in
Google Cloud called IoT Core.

00:43:24.250 --> 00:43:26.380
And what it does
is it allows you

00:43:26.380 --> 00:43:28.930
to communicate between a
device in the cloud securely.

00:43:28.930 --> 00:43:31.100
It connects it in a
secure way to the cloud.

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:34.678
And now that physical device
has a digital representation.

00:43:34.678 --> 00:43:36.970
In the industry, they sometimes
call it a digital twin.

00:43:36.970 --> 00:43:39.310
They have this digital
object in the cloud

00:43:39.310 --> 00:43:40.900
that you can kind
of interact with.

00:43:40.900 --> 00:43:43.180
And there's APIs,
where, depending

00:43:43.180 --> 00:43:44.620
on how you want
to trigger it, you

00:43:44.620 --> 00:43:48.490
can send admin messages,
configuration messages

00:43:48.490 --> 00:43:51.500
or commands, back down to your
device through this connection.

00:43:51.500 --> 00:43:53.170
So when the device
is online, you

00:43:53.170 --> 00:43:55.450
can basically say
things like, OK, I

00:43:55.450 --> 00:43:58.720
want a fan to automatically turn
on when the temperature hits

00:43:58.720 --> 00:43:59.540
a certain level.

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:01.472
So it's easy if
they're in proximity.

00:44:01.472 --> 00:44:03.430
We've all got a thermostat
in the house, right?

00:44:03.430 --> 00:44:05.440
So the thermostat
hits a certain level,

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:08.110
and either your heater or
your AC comes on in the house.

00:44:08.110 --> 00:44:10.420
Same concept, and now
you can divorce it

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:11.870
from being physically connected.

00:44:11.870 --> 00:44:13.300
So you don't have to have
a thermostat connected

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:14.410
to your heater system anymore.

00:44:14.410 --> 00:44:15.880
The thermostat's
connected to the internet.

00:44:15.880 --> 00:44:17.500
Your heater is connected
to the internet.

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:19.690
And now based on the
temperature on your thermostat,

00:44:19.690 --> 00:44:21.430
your furnace can come on.

00:44:21.430 --> 00:44:23.160
So this is the
same idea of, Nest

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:25.660
does this with the smart stuff
and sending all the data out.

00:44:25.660 --> 00:44:28.310
That's kind of the same
idea of how you do this.

00:44:28.310 --> 00:44:30.010
So from a physical
standpoint, you've

00:44:30.010 --> 00:44:33.738
got some input, and that can
be, like temperature going up

00:44:33.738 --> 00:44:34.280
to the cloud.

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:36.595
And those can be messages
going into Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:44:36.595 --> 00:44:38.970
And then you've got something
watching Cloud Pub/Sub that

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:42.430
reads those messages and sends
through the IoT Core admin

00:44:42.430 --> 00:44:45.610
interface that says, for
this device, do this.

00:44:45.610 --> 00:44:47.768
And then IoT Core reaches
out to that device

00:44:47.768 --> 00:44:49.810
through the digital
representation that it's got,

00:44:49.810 --> 00:44:51.220
and the device responds to it.

00:44:51.220 --> 00:44:52.720
So it's a pretty simple--

00:44:52.720 --> 00:44:55.060
by APIs, I mean, it's
like one function call.

00:44:55.060 --> 00:44:56.830
It's not like there's
huge amounts of code

00:44:56.830 --> 00:44:57.280
you have to write.

00:44:57.280 --> 00:44:58.290
It's really concise.

00:44:58.290 --> 00:44:58.980
It's nice.

00:44:58.980 --> 00:45:01.120
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So IoT Core
does a lot of the work there,

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:02.680
and it kind of does
two different parts.

00:45:02.680 --> 00:45:04.360
It does the part where it
receives a signal, which

00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:06.290
I think you can call through
Pub/Sub or some other Cloud

00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:08.230
Functions, which,
hopefully, most people who

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:10.700
are working in the cloud have
a good sense of how to do.

00:45:10.700 --> 00:45:13.722
And then on the IoT Core side,
it also creates a digital

00:45:13.722 --> 00:45:15.430
version-- like you
said, a digital twin--

00:45:15.430 --> 00:45:17.250
that basically, you're
sending signals to that.

00:45:17.250 --> 00:45:18.730
And then when that
gets the signal,

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:21.155
it handles the mechanical
part of connecting that

00:45:21.155 --> 00:45:22.030
to the actual device.

00:45:22.030 --> 00:45:24.490
GABE WEISS: So my product
manager is going to freak out.

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:27.760
Digital twin has a very specific
meaning in a lot of places.

00:45:27.760 --> 00:45:30.573
And this is not technically
like what the industry calls--

00:45:30.573 --> 00:45:32.740
I think of it as digital
twin because I haven't been

00:45:32.740 --> 00:45:34.090
in the IT industry for decades.

00:45:34.090 --> 00:45:37.485
But people that have been, there
is a very specific connotation.

00:45:37.485 --> 00:45:38.110
I call it that.

00:45:38.110 --> 00:45:41.110
But it's really just an object
in the cloud that represents

00:45:41.110 --> 00:45:42.580
this physical device.

00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:45.460
I wrote a blog post about
literal step-by-step

00:45:45.460 --> 00:45:47.390
if you wanted to do
something like this.

00:45:47.390 --> 00:45:48.670
I'm Gabe Weiss on medium.com.

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:50.670
If you just look for Gabe
Weiss, you'll find it.

00:45:50.670 --> 00:45:52.253
The show notes will
have a link to it.

00:45:52.253 --> 00:45:54.338
But yeah, I basically
came from the concept of,

00:45:54.338 --> 00:45:56.380
in the IoT world, there's
two types of engineers.

00:45:56.380 --> 00:45:58.463
There's hardware engineers
and software engineers.

00:45:58.463 --> 00:46:01.210
And the hardware engineers often
have no idea about the cloud.

00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:02.950
This is a broad generalization.

00:46:02.950 --> 00:46:04.000
It's not always true.

00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:06.805
But Silicon hardware
people often

00:46:06.805 --> 00:46:07.930
don't understand the cloud.

00:46:07.930 --> 00:46:10.000
And then cloud people
and then software people

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:12.620
often have no idea about
the physical devices.

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:14.853
So I came at it from a
let's pretend no knowledge.

00:46:14.853 --> 00:46:16.270
Let's pretend that
people that are

00:46:16.270 --> 00:46:18.310
coming to wanting
to do this have

00:46:18.310 --> 00:46:19.730
no idea how to do anything.

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:22.570
So it's step-by-step,
very clear--

00:46:22.570 --> 00:46:23.320
run this command.

00:46:23.320 --> 00:46:25.253
Make sure you see this output.

00:46:25.253 --> 00:46:26.920
The blog posts I wrote
for this are very

00:46:26.920 --> 00:46:29.132
detailed on how to do that.

00:46:29.132 --> 00:46:31.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So if
